The test reuses the same `ByteBuf payload` across all loop iterations without resetting its reader index. After the first `writeInbound(payload)` call, the handler's `writeBytes(payload)` consumes the buffer (advances reader index to writer index). On every subsequent iteration, `packet.readableBytes()` will be `0`, so no meaningful data is written and the sequence numbers barely advance.
The assertion `bytesWritten.get()).isGreaterThan(2 * (fourGB + 1000))` will fail because only ~65 KB is actually written per direction.
Fix: create a fresh buffer each iteration, or reset the reader index:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < ((fourGB / chunkSize) + 2); i++) {
    payload.readerIndex(0);
    embeddedChannel.writeInbound(payload);
}
```
(or use `payload.duplicate()` / `payload.copy()` per iteration).
